1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,908
Your backtest and your live system are usually two different codebases.

2
00:00:04,008 --> 00:00:05,560
That's why they drift.

3
00:00:05,917 --> 00:00:08,084
You describe it once, in Rust.

4
00:00:08,184 --> 00:00:12,964
For example: an order book, fanned out to each side, recombined into a quote.

5
00:00:13,750 --> 00:00:15,697
Under the hood it's a graph.

6
00:00:15,797 --> 00:00:19,775
Each node runs once per cycle, even where branches recombine.

7
00:00:19,875 --> 00:00:26,512
Nitro compiles it: a hundred-node benchmark graph runs a cycle in thirty-two nanoseconds.

8
00:00:27,117 --> 00:00:30,500
Historically: an instant, deterministic replay.

9
00:00:30,550 --> 00:00:32,485
Run the same graph live.

10
00:00:32,585 --> 00:00:34,323
Paced by the wall clock.

11
00:00:34,423 --> 00:00:36,288
Same values, same order.

12
00:00:36,388 --> 00:00:38,300
Only the clock changes.

13
00:00:38,350 --> 00:00:39,937
One definition.

14
00:00:40,037 --> 00:00:41,728
Two run modes.

15
00:00:41,828 --> 00:00:44,158
The implementations can't drift.

16
00:00:44,450 --> 00:00:45,874
Wingfoil.

17
00:00:45,974 --> 00:00:48,127
Stream processing in Rust.
